 "literal": "Wind-like fast going with horses / flag-waving on top those with chariots, saying so / ocean-like seeing bright weapons with army / mountain-against fighting those with male elephants, saying so / thunder-like raging fear-coming with drum / battle-excelling those of victory, saying so / earth-having army bright ornaments kings / white umbrella wealth admiring we do not / by us those who are admired / placed thorn garden young goat having grazed left behind / short fragrant munjai thick bud short greens / dry land of millet with boiled rice receiving / small village kings even though towards us / greatness having known behaving those of good character / much great distress even if experiencing even a little / understanding those who do not have wealth we do not think of / good wisdom those who possess poverty / we will think of, O Great One, we having rejoiced very greatly",
 "translation": "We do not admire the white-umbrellaed wealth of kings who possess bright ornaments and armies covering the earth, even if they have horses that run like the wind, chariots with waving flags, bright weapons like the ocean, male elephants that fight like clashing mountains, and terrifying drums that sound like thunder, and are victorious in battle. Those whom we admire are the chiefs of small villages who, though they eat only simple short greens of the fragrant munjai shrub left behind by grazing goats in thorn-fenced backyards along with boiled millet from dry lands, understand our worth and treat us with respect. O Great One! Even if we suffer immense distress, we do not think of the wealth of those who lack understanding. We will always think of and greatly cherish the poverty of those who possess good wisdom.",
 "modern": "காற்றுப் போல வேகமாகச் செல்லும் குதிரைகளையும், கொடிகள் அசையும் தேர்களையும், கடல் போன்ற ஒளிரும் படைகளையும், மலைகளோடு போரிடும் யானைகளையும், இடி போன்ற முரசுகளையும் கொண்டு போரில் வெல்லும் வேந்தர்களின் வெண்கொற்றக்குடைச் செல்வத்தை நாங்கள் மதிப்பதில்லை. முள்வேலி சூழ்ந்த தோட்டத்தில் ஆடுகள் மேய்ந்து எஞ்சிய முஞ்ஞைக் கீரையையும், புன்புல வரகின் சோற்றையும் உண்ணும் எளிய சிற்றூர் மன்னர்களாயினும், எம்முடைய தகுதியை அறிந்து போற்றும் பண்புடையோரை மட்டுமே நாங்கள் மதிப்போம். பெருமானே! நாங்கள் எவ்வளவு பெரிய துன்பத்தை அடைந்தாலும், அறிவில்லாதவர்களின் செல்வத்தை நினைக்கமாட்டோம். நல்லறிவு உடையோரின் வறுமையையே நாங்கள் மகிழ்ந்து பெரிதும் போற்றுவோம்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Kōnāṭṭu Eṟiccalūr Māṭalaṉ Maturai Kumaraṉār addresses the Chola king Kurāppaḷḷi Tuñciya Peruntirumāvaḷavaṉ in this poem of the pāṭāṇ tiṇai. He contrasts the immense material wealth of mighty kings with the moral and intellectual character of small chieftains. Even if great kings possess vast armies, fast horses, and mighty elephants, their wealth is worthless to the poet if they lack empathy and understanding. On the other hand, the poet highly esteems the small chiefs of tiny villages who, despite eating simple millet and wild greens, recognize the value of poets and treat them with dignity. The key words are 'உணர்ச்சி இல்லோர் உடைமை உள்ளேம்' (we do not think of the wealth of those without understanding) and 'நல்லறிவு உடையோர் நல்குரவு உள்ளுதும்' (we cherish the poverty of those with good wisdom).",
